<DocsLayout title="Portable training" description="One scmle job spec, a runner interface: durable run ids, checkpoints, retries, cost budgets, separated training and serving roles, and what has not been run." current="portable-training">
  <p class="eyebrow"><span></span> Serve and operate</p>
  <h1>One job spec,<br /><em>any place to run it.</em></h1>
  <p class="lead">A <code>JobSpec</code> and a <code>Runner</code> interface. The package ships the local runner, which runs and is tested; a runner for any other place is written by the user against the same interface.</p>

  <h2>The spec and the run</h2>
  <p>A <code>JobSpec</code> names a durable <code>run_id</code> (the idempotency key), the project specification (opaque JSON), the training identity, resources, a timeout, a <code>RetryPolicy</code>, an optional <code>CostBudget</code> and a non-secret environment. A GPU needs a stated <code>gpu_reason</code>: specialised compute only behind a declared need. <code>Runner.run(spec)</code> behaves the same on every runner:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The spec is stored at <code>runs/&lt;id&gt;/spec.json</code>. The same id with a different spec is a <code>RunConflictError</code>; a finished id returns its recorded manifest and starts nothing.</li>
    <li><code>runs/&lt;id&gt;/manifest.json</code> is rewritten atomically at every transition: attempts with state, seconds, cost and reason; checkpoint digests; result digest; evidence list; final state.</li>
    <li>An attempt that ends <strong>interrupted</strong> (preemption, spot reclaim, SIGTERM) or <strong>failed with a retryable cause</strong> is retried after the policy's backoff, up to its attempt limit, and the retry resumes from the run's checkpoints. Other failures, timeouts and budget stops are final.</li>
    <li>The <strong>cost budget</strong> is the declared rate times resources times elapsed time, accumulated across attempts. Past the ceiling the job is cancelled and the run ends <code>budget_stopped</code>. It is an estimate from declared rates, not billing data.</li>
    <li>Final states: <code>succeeded</code>, <code>failed</code>, <code>budget_stopped</code>, <code>timed_out</code>, <code>retries_exhausted</code>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Workloads save progress with <code>context.step(n, state)</code> and read it back with <code>context.resume()</code>. Each checkpoint carries its own digest; a damaged newest one is skipped and the one before it is used.</p>

  <h2>Roles</h2>
  <p>Training and serving are different identities with different permissions. Training reads <code>data/</code>, <code>runs/</code> and <code>artifacts/</code> and writes <code>runs/</code>, <code>artifacts/</code> and <code>evidence/</code>. Serving reads <code>bundles/</code> and <code>artifacts/</code> and writes <code>audit/</code>. A separation check refuses overlapping write prefixes, a training role that can write <code>bundles/</code> and a serving role that can write <code>runs/</code>. Workloads receive a <code>RoleStore</code> that enforces the training role, and a job spec naming the serving identity is refused by every runner. Releasing a bundle is a third act, with an approval, outside both.</p>

  <h2>Runners</h2>
  <DocTable label="Runners" head={["Runner", "Runs the attempt as", "Identity", "Interruption"]} rows={adapters} />
  <p>A runner for a container platform starts the runner entrypoint in the image. It opens the <code>file://</code> store named by <code>SCMLE_STORE_URI</code>, loads the stored spec, refuses it if its digest is not the one the runner started, and runs the workload named by <code>SCMLE_WORKLOAD</code>. The default workload runs an unchanged <code>MLProject</code>: it copies the data from the store, mirrors the run directory to <code>runs/&lt;id&gt;/workdir/</code> every 30 seconds and at the end, and after a restart on an empty disk restores it and calls <code>MLProject.resume</code>. A hard kill loses at most the trials since the last mirror.</p>
  <p>A reproducibility report compares each metric of a result against tolerances declared before the runs; a miss is published, not hidden.</p>

  <h2>What was not done</h2>
  <p>Only the local runner ships and is tested. No container image was built, no job was interrupted on a remote platform, and no cost was measured against a bill. The reproducibility-tolerance report against a second place does not exist.</p>

  <p class="meta">Training runners of the 0.1 beta line</p>
</DocsLayout>
